import history from "../../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../../data/standards/ifric/ifric22.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType="IFRS"
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.summary}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope & Definitions" },
        { id: "s3", label: "The Question IFRIC 22 Answers" },
        { id: "s4", label: "The Core Conclusion — Date of Initial Recognition" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Multiple Advance Payments or Receipts" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Why the Prepayment Is Non-Monetary" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Worked Example — Single Advance Payment" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Worked Example — Instalment Advances" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Disclosure" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Judgments & Estimates" },
        { id: "s11", label: "IFRIC 22 vs US GAAP" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>

  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Why the exchange rate for advance consideration was unclear</div>
        <div class="principle">IAS 21's general rule is that foreign currency transactions are translated at the EXCHANGE RATE AT THE DATE OF THE TRANSACTION. But advance consideration (prepayments or advance receipts) creates a timing gap: the CURRENCY EXCHANGE OCCURS at one date, while the RELATED REVENUE OR EXPENSE is recognised at a LATER DATE. IFRIC 22 resolves this gap by clarifying that the "date of the transaction" for translation purposes is the DATE OF INITIAL RECOGNITION of the prepayment or deferred income — not the later date when revenue or expense is recognised.</div>
        <div class="note">This clarification was important because advance consideration is common in international trade, construction, and service industries — entities were applying different exchange rates, creating inconsistent measurement of both the prepayment and the related revenue/expense.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">The monetary vs non-monetary distinction</div>
        <div class="principle">IAS 21 distinguishes between MONETARY and NON-MONETARY items for exchange rate translation. Monetary items are retranslated at each reporting date; non-monetary items are translated at the transaction date and never retranslated. IFRIC 22 clarifies that ADVANCE CONSIDERATION is a NON-MONETARY item — it is not retranslated at each reporting date, but is measured at the exchange rate on the date of initial recognition.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope & Definitions</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">What IFRIC 22 covers</div>
        <div class="principle">IFRIC 22 applies to the EXCHANGE RATE used when translating ADVANCE CONSIDERATION — including ADVANCE PAYMENTS (prepayments made by the entity) and ADVANCE RECEIPTS (deferred income from customers). The interpretation applies when the advance consideration is denominated in a FOREIGN CURRENCY and the entity's functional currency is different. IFRIC 22 does NOT apply to monetary items (which are retranslated under IAS 21's general rule) or to the subsequent measurement of the related revenue or expense.</div>
        <div class="elabel">TYPICAL FACT PATTERNS</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>Advance payments for imported inventory paid in USD when functional currency is GBP</span>
          <span>Advance receipts from international customers denominated in EUR for services to be delivered later</span>
          <span>Construction contracts with advance payments in foreign currency</span>
          <span>Prepayments for PPE acquired from a foreign supplier</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Key definitions</div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Advance consideration</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">An amount paid or received in advance of the delivery of goods or services, denominated in a foreign currency, which gives rise to a prepayment asset or deferred income liability.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;">
          <div class="glossary-term">Date of initial recognition</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">The date on which the entity first recognises the prepayment asset or deferred income liability — the exchange rate date IFRIC 22 specifies for translation.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>The Question IFRIC 22 Answers</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">The specific ambiguity IFRIC 22 resolved</div>
        <div class="principle">Before IFRIC 22, entities faced a genuine ambiguity: IAS 21 says to translate at the "date of the transaction," but advance consideration involves TWO dates — the date the currency is EXCHANGED and the date the PREPAYMENT OR DEFERRED INCOME is RECOGNISED. Different entities applied different dates, leading to materially different measurements. Some used the exchange date (when the cash changed hands); others used the later recognition date (when the prepayment or deferred income was recorded). IFRIC 22 resolves this by specifying the RECOGNITION DATE.</div>
        <div class="callout warn callout--flush">⚠️ The "date of the transaction" in IAS 21 refers to the date the entity ACQUIRES or INCURS the obligation — for advance consideration, that is the date of INITIAL RECOGNITION of the prepayment or deferred income, NOT the date the cash was paid or received.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>The Core Conclusion — Date of Initial Recognition</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">The definitive rule</div>
        <div class="principle">IFRIC 22 specifies that advance consideration is translated at the EXCHANGE RATE AT THE DATE OF INITIAL RECOGNITION of the prepayment asset or deferred income liability. This date is when the entity FIRST RECOGNISES the asset or liability in its financial statements — which may be the date the cash is paid or received, but may be a different date if the entity's accounting policies defer recognition.</div>
        <div class="formula-box">
          <div class="f-label">TRANSLATION RATE FOR ADVANCE CONSIDERATION</div>
          <div class="f-eq">Advance consideration (functional currency) = Foreign currency amount × Exchange rate at the date of initial recognition</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Why the recognition date, not the cash flow date</div>
        <div class="principle">The recognition date is the appropriate date because it is the point at which the entity ACQUIRES A RIGHT (prepayment) or INCURS AN OBLIGATION (deferred income). At that point, the economic substance of the transaction is complete — the entity has either paid for future goods/services or received payment for future delivery. The cash flow date is merely the settlement mechanism; the accounting event occurs at recognition.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Multiple Advance Payments or Receipts</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Each advance payment has its own exchange rate</div>
        <div class="principle">When an entity makes MULTIPLE ADVANCE PAYMENTS or receives multiple advance receipts, EACH PAYMENT is translated at the EXCHANGE RATE ON ITS OWN DATE OF INITIAL RECOGNITION. There is no averaging or pooling of exchange rates across multiple payments. This means that if a company makes advance payments on 1 January, 1 March, and 1 June, each payment is translated at the rate on its respective date.</div>
        <div class="elabel">PRACTICAL IMPLICATIONS</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>Each prepayment/receipt creates a separate asset/liability measured at its own exchange rate</span>
          <span>When the advance is applied to revenue or expense, the translation difference (if any) affects the P&L</span>
          <span>Systems must track individual advance payments by date — not just aggregate amounts</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Why the Prepayment Is Non-Monetary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">The non-monetary classification</div>
        <div class="principle">IFRIC 22 clarifies that ADVANCE CONSIDERATION is a NON-MONETARY ITEM under IAS 21. This means it is NOT retranslated at each reporting date using the closing rate. Instead, it is measured at the exchange rate on the date of initial recognition and remains at that rate until the prepayment is applied to revenue or expense. This classification is consistent with IAS 21's general principle that non-monetary items are measured at historical cost (transaction date rate).</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">The contrast with monetary items</div>
        <div class="principle">If the advance consideration WERE a monetary item, it would be retranslated at each reporting date using the closing rate, with exchange differences recognised in profit or loss. IFRIC 22's non-monetary classification means that exchange differences arise ONLY when the prepayment is ultimately applied to revenue or expense — not at each interim reporting date. This produces less P&L volatility than a monetary treatment would.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Worked Example — Single Advance Payment</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="worked">
          <div class="wk-title">Example: USD advance payment for inventory</div>
          <div class="wk-body">A UK company (functional currency: GBP) pays a USD 100,000 advance to a US supplier for inventory to be delivered in 6 months. The exchange rate on the payment date (1 January) is 1.25 USD/GBP. The exchange rate on the delivery date (30 June) is 1.35 USD/GBP. The inventory cost is USD 500,000 in total.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer"><strong>1 January — Advance payment recognised:</strong><br>Prepayment = USD 100,000 ÷ 1.25 = £80,000<br>Exchange rate used: 1.25 (date of initial recognition)<br><br><strong>30 June — Inventory delivered:</strong><br>Total inventory cost = USD 500,000<br>Balance paid = USD 400,000 ÷ 1.35 = £296,296<br>Total inventory cost = £80,000 + £296,296 = £376,296<br><br><strong>Exchange difference:</strong><br>No retranslation of the prepayment at 30 June (non-monetary)<br>The prepayment is simply applied to the inventory cost at its original rate of £80,000<br><br><strong>Key point:</strong> The prepayment is translated ONLY at 1.25 (the recognition date). It is NOT retranslated at 1.35 on 30 June. The exchange difference (£80,000 vs £74,074 at 1.35) is NOT recognised in P&L — it is simply part of the inventory cost.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Worked Example — Instalment Advances</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="worked">
          <div class="wk-title">Example: Three instalment advance payments</div>
          <div class="wk-body">A German company (functional currency: EUR) makes three advance payments for equipment from a Japanese supplier: EUR 50,000 on 1 February (rate: 130 JPY/EUR), EUR 30,000 on 1 April (rate: 135 JPY/EUR), and EUR 20,000 on 1 June (rate: 140 JPY/EUR). Total equipment cost is JPY 15,000,000, delivered on 1 July.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer"><strong>1 February payment:</strong><br>Prepayment 1 = EUR 50,000<br>Rate: 130 JPY/EUR<br><br><strong>1 April payment:</strong><br>Prepayment 2 = EUR 30,000<br>Rate: 135 JPY/EUR<br><br><strong>1 June payment:</strong><br>Prepayment 3 = EUR 20,000<br>Rate: 140 JPY/EUR<br><br><strong>1 July — Equipment delivered:</strong><br>Total prepayments = EUR 100,000<br>Remaining balance = JPY 15,000,000 - (50,000×130 + 30,000×135 + 20,000×140)<br>= JPY 15,000,000 - JPY 13,100,000 = JPY 1,900,000<br>Balance in EUR = JPY 1,900,000 ÷ 140 = EUR 13,571<br><br><strong>Total equipment cost:</strong><br>EUR 50,000 + EUR 30,000 + EUR 20,000 + EUR 13,571 = EUR 113,571<br><br><strong>Key point:</strong> Each advance payment is translated at its OWN rate. No averaging across payments. The equipment is recognised at the sum of the translated advances plus the translated balance payment.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">What IFRIC 22 requires</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>The exchange rate(s) used to translate advance consideration</span>
          <span>The carrying amount of advance consideration at the beginning and end of the period</span>
          <span>Any exchange differences recognised when the advance consideration is applied to revenue or expense</span>
          <span>Whether the advance consideration relates to inventory, PPE, or revenue recognition</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Judgments & Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Determining the exact date of initial recognition for advance consideration (especially when there is a delay between payment and accounting recognition)</span>
              <span>Whether the advance consideration is monetary or non-monetary (affecting retranslation requirements)</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>The timing of the related revenue or expense recognition, which determines when the advance is derecognised</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>IFRIC 22 vs US GAAP</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — IFRIC 22 vs US GAAP</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-12-1-1"><span>Feature</span><span>IFRIC 22 (IFRS)</span><span>US GAAP</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Translation date</span><span>Date of initial recognition of prepayment/deferred income</span><span>Transaction date (when the foreign currency transaction occurs)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-12-1-1"><span>Retranslation</span><span>Non-monetary — not retranslated at each reporting date</span><span>Non-monetary — not retranslated at each reporting date</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Multiple payments</span><span>Each payment translated at its own date of initial recognition</span><span>Each payment at its own transaction date</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-12-1-1"><span>Exchange differences</span><span>Recognised when the advance is applied to revenue or expense</span><span>Recognised when the advance is applied to revenue or expense</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Where every IFRIC 22 event lands</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Advance payment recognised (prepayment asset)</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Non-monetary asset at exchange rate on recognition date</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>Advance receipt recognised (deferred income)</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Non-monetary liability at exchange rate on recognition date</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Prepayment applied to inventory/PPE</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span> <span class="badge bp">SOPL</span></span><span>Asset recognised at translated amount; no exchange difference in P&L</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>Deferred income recognised as revenue</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span> <span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Revenue at translated amount; any difference vs cash received in deferred income</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>IAS 21</span><span>The parent standard IFRIC 22 interprets, supplying the monetary/non-monetary distinction and general transaction-date translation rule</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>IFRS 15</span><span>Governs recognition of the related revenue for advance consideration received from customers</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>IAS 2</span><span>Governs recognition of inventory acquired using advance consideration</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>IAS 16</span><span>Governs recognition of PPE acquired using advance consideration</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Quick-reference glossary</div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Advance consideration</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">An amount paid or received in advance of the delivery of goods or services, denominated in a foreign currency, which gives rise to a prepayment asset or deferred income liability.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;">
          <div class="glossary-term">Date of initial recognition</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">The date on which the entity first recognises the prepayment asset or deferred income liability — the exchange rate date IFRIC 22 specifies for translation.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <Glossary entries={[
      { term: "Advance consideration", definition: "An amount paid or received in advance of the delivery of goods or services, denominated in a foreign currency, which gives rise to a prepayment asset or deferred income liability under IFRIC 22." },
      { term: "Date of initial recognition", definition: "The date on which the entity first recognises the prepayment asset or deferred income liability — the exchange rate date IFRIC 22 specifies for translation." },
      { term: "Non-monetary item", definition: "An item that is not a right to receive a fixed or determinable number of units of currency — advance consideration is classified as non-monetary, meaning it is not retranslated at each reporting date." }
    ]} />

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.IFRIC22} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>




